<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Savra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1385">1385</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Savra was fought on 18 September 1385 near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushnje">Lushnjë</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. An Ottoman army, called in by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a>, defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a>, the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, who had seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>. The battle left many Albanian lords as Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and marked a major step in the Ottoman advance into Albania.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle is also called the Battle of the Vjosë, and in Serbian the battle on the Saurian field. In 1372 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsa-ii">Balša II</a> married Comita Muzaka, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-ii-muzaka">Andrea II Muzaka</a>, and gained as her dowry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanine">Kaninë</a> and Himarë, the Principality of Vlorë. He later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>, presumably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a>, and styled himself Duke of Durrës in a charter for Ragusa. His expansion towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> put him on the front line against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and aware of their designs on his lands he asked the Venetians on 8 August 1385 for the help of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-thopia">Karl Thopia</a> invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to support him against Balša, and Hayreddin Pasha brought his forces from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> to the Savra field near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushnje">Lushnjë</a>. Balša heard of the incursion into the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a>. According to Mavro Orbini, he gathered 1,000 men and, against the advice of his nobles, marched out against the much larger Ottoman force. His small army had little success, and he was killed; Orbini writes that his body was beheaded and the head taken to Hayreddin Pasha.</p><p>Orbini alone names Ivaniš Mrnjavčević among the combatants, and some scholars doubt that he existed, while others see him as a loyal member of the Balša family. Orbini also tells of Balša's voivode Đurađ Krvavčić, a brave warrior who died in the battle. His work The Realm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> is one of the main sources for the battle but contains much that is wrong or imprecise; Marin Barleti, the other, describes Balša as brave and idealistic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Most of the local Serbian and Albanian lords became Ottoman vassals. Thopia recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> at once, probably under Ottoman suzerainty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulcinj">Ulcinj</a> but soon withdrew, keeping only Kastoria. The historian Stojan Novaković held that for these lords the battle mattered as much as the battles of Marica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> together, and it brought an influx of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> into the Ottoman forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Savra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Balša II seized Durrës in 1385, and Karl called in the Ottomans, who killed Balša at <b>Savra</b>; Karl recovered the city soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-albania-medieval">Principality of Albania (medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman pressure grew after their victory at <b>Savra</b> in 1385.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-berat">Principality of Berat</a></li><li><blockquote>Balša II took Valona and Durrës, but died fighting the Ottomans at <b>Savra</b> in 1385.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a></li></ul></section>
